    q: 'What is X-linked protoporphyria?',
    a: 'X-linked protoporphyria (XLP) is a rare inherited porphyria that looks like erythropoietic protoporphyria: burning pain within minutes of visible light. The enzyme defect is the opposite of EPP. EPP cannot finish heme. XLP’s erythroid ALAS2 is overactive, so protoporphyrin IX is made faster than it can be cleared.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How common is it among people who look like they have EPP?',
    a: 'On the order of 2 to 10 percent of people with the EPP phenotype turn out to have XLP when the gene is sequenced. A laboratory clue is zinc-chelated protoporphyrin making up a large share of the red-cell protoporphyrin, often cited around 15 to 50 percent, instead of almost all of it being metal-free.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does the approved melanocortin drug cover XLP?',
    a: 'The US label for afamelanotide names erythropoietic protoporphyria, not XLP. The pigment strategy does not care which gene overproduced the porphyrin: MC1R still builds eumelanin that absorbs light. The oral MC1R agonist dersimelagon was studied in EPP and XLP together, and that Phase 3 trial met its primary endpoint. It is not approved.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is XLP a melanocortin disease?',
    a: 'No. The disease is a heme-pathway disease. Melanocortin drugs are a photoprotective workaround. They do not slow ALAS2 or clear protoporphyrin, and they do not treat the liver injury that accumulated protoporphyrin can cause in a minority of people.',
  },
];

