<PageLayout
  title="X-linked protoporphyria: the same light pain, from too much heme precursor"
  seoTitle="X-linked protoporphyria (XLP) and melanocortins"
  kicker="The condition"
  description="X-linked protoporphyria causes the same burning pain in visible light as erythropoietic protoporphyria, but the enzyme error runs the other way: ALAS2 is overactive, so protoporphyrin IX is overproduced. It is a minority of the EPP phenotype. Afamelanotide’s US label names EPP. The oral MC1R agonist dersimelagon was tested in both, and that Phase 3 trial met its primary endpoint."
  seoDescription="XLP is protoporphyrin pain from an overactive ALAS2, not a weak ferrochelatase. The US afamelanotide label names EPP; dersimelagon’s Phase 3 included both."
  thread="Two genes, one phototoxic molecule. The melanocortin drug does not care which gene made the protoporphyrin. It only adds pigment in front of the light."
  updated="2026"
  about={[
    { name: 'X-linked protoporphyria', type: 'MedicalCondition', sameAs: ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/X-linked_protoporphyria'] },
    { name: 'Melanocortin 1 receptor', type: 'MedicalEntity', sameAs: ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melanocortin_1_receptor'] },
  ]}
>
  <KeyTakeaways
    items={[
      'X-linked protoporphyria (XLP) is clinically the same light disease as erythropoietic protoporphyria: burning pain within minutes of visible violet-blue light.',
      'The biochemistry is the opposite error. EPP cannot insert iron into protoporphyrin IX. XLP’s ALAS2 is a gain-of-function, so the pathway makes protoporphyrin faster than a normal ferrochelatase can finish it.',
      'About 2 to 10 percent of people with the EPP phenotype have XLP. A high fraction of zinc-protoporphyrin in red cells is a clue before the gene test.',
      'The US afamelanotide label names EPP, not XLP. Dersimelagon’s Phase 3 trial enrolled both and met its primary endpoint in 2026; the drug is not approved.',
      'Neither drug fixes ALAS2 or clears protoporphyrin. Both are a pigment workaround. Liver injury from the same molecule remains a separate problem.',
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    People with <strong>X-linked protoporphyria</strong> describe the same illness as people
    with <a href="/epp">erythropoietic protoporphyria</a>: pain in the skin within minutes of
    daylight, including light through window glass, often with nothing a bystander can see.
    For years the two were not separated. They still share the photosensitiser, protoporphyrin
    IX. They do not share the broken step.
  </p>

  <h2>Too much of the first enzyme, not too little of the last</h2>
  <p>
    Heme in red-cell precursors starts with ALAS2, the erythroid form of
    5-aminolevulinate synthase, and ends when ferrochelatase drops an iron into
    protoporphyrin IX. EPP is a failure of that last insertion, usually from a bad
    ferrochelatase allele opposite a common low-expression allele. XLP, described in 2008,
    is a gain of function at the start: small deletions that remove the C-terminus of
    ALAS2, so the enzyme runs too fast. Protoporphyrin is produced beyond what a normal
    ferrochelatase can clear. There is no anaemia and no iron overload of the kind other
    ALAS2 diseases cause.<Cite n={1} />
  </p>
  <p>
    The inheritance is X-linked. Hemizygous males and heterozygous females can both be
    affected; a woman’s disease can be as severe as a male relative’s, or she can be nearly
    silent. “Erythropoietic protoporphyria, X-linked dominant” is a name that flattens that
    range, which is why the condition is called XLP.<Cite n={1} />
  </p>
  <p>
    Among people who look as if they have EPP, sequencing finds XLP in about 2 to 10
    percent.<Cite n={2} /> A practical clue sits in the red cell before the gene result.
    In EPP almost all of the protoporphyrin is metal-free. In XLP a large share is
    zinc-chelated, on the order of 15 to 50 percent of the total.<Cite n={2} /> Same pain,
    different fraction, and a reason not to stop at a clinical label of “EPP.”
  </p>

  <h2>Why a melanocortin drug is even relevant</h2>
  <p>
    Nothing about ALAS2 is a melanocortin receptor. The drug rationale is downstream of the
    light, not downstream of the gene. Protoporphyrin absorbs hardest around 400–410 nm and
    hands that energy to oxygen. <a href="/receptors/mc1r">MC1R</a> agonism builds eumelanin,
    which sits in the skin and intercepts light before it reaches the vessel. That is the
    whole of the <a href="/afamelanotide">afamelanotide</a> idea, and it does not require the
    porphyrin to have come from a weak ferrochelatase.
  </p>
  <p>
    The regulatory wording is narrower than the mechanism. The US label for afamelanotide
    is to increase pain-free light exposure in adults with phototoxic reactions from
    erythropoietic protoporphyria.<Cite n={3} /> It names EPP. It does not name XLP. The
    oral small-molecule MC1R agonist <a href="/dersimelagon">dersimelagon</a> was studied in
    a Phase 3 trial that enrolled both, and the sponsor reported in 2026 that the trial met
    its primary endpoint — time in sunlight before the first warning symptom.<Cite n={4} />
    That result is a company announcement until the paper is out, and the drug is not
    approved. Full trial numbers and the regulatory path are on the dersimelagon page.
  </p>

  <h2>What pigment does not fix</h2>
  <p>
    A tan does not slow ALAS2 and does not remove protoporphyrin from blood or liver. A
    minority of people with protoporphyria develop liver injury because the liver is where
    the excess porphyrin is excreted and where it can precipitate. That risk belongs to the
    molecule, so it belongs to XLP as well as to EPP, and it is outside what an MC1R agonist
    is for.<Cite n={2} /> Avoiding the triggering wavelengths, and the specialist care of the
    porphyria itself, are not replaced by a melanocortin.
  </p>

  <h2>The honest bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    XLP is the gain-of-function twin of EPP: too much precursor rather than too little
    finishing enzyme, the same phototoxic product, and the same reason a pigment drug can
    help without being a cure. For the shared illness, see <a href="/epp">EPP</a>; for the
    two drugs, <a href="/afamelanotide">afamelanotide</a> and{' '}
    <a href="/dersimelagon">dersimelagon</a>; for the receptor,{' '}
    <a href="/receptors/mc1r">MC1R</a>.
  </p>

  <div class="aside-note not-prose">
    <p class="font-medium text-[var(--text)]">Education, not a protocol</p>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[var(--text)]">
      This page explains X-linked protoporphyria and why melanocortin drugs are studied in
      it. It is not medical advice and gives no dosing or treatment instructions. See the{' '}
      <a href="/about">editorial standards</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

